<%# THE STRUCTURAL CHECK (spec §8's three lines and §9's gate), and the form that makes it
    computable at all. Local: `presenter` (BudgetPagePresenter).

    WHY THE FORM LIVES HERE and not on the account settings page: `typical_income`,
    `period_cadence` and `period_anchor_date` had a column, a validation and a reader each and no
    writer anywhere in the app — the whole periods system ran on seed data. They are declared
    beside the figures they produce, because the figures are the only reason to declare them, and
    a user who disagrees with a number here can correct its input without leaving the number.

    Below the fill order, not above it: §8 puts the rules first and the check under them, and the
    check is a reading OF those rules. %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" data-structural-check>
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Does this fit your income?</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="px-6 py-5 space-y-5">
    <% if presenter.declared? %>
      <%# §8's three lines, verbatim in shape: the claim, the income, the difference. A definition
          list rather than a table — three labelled figures are not tabular data, and `dt`/`dd`
          gives a screen reader the pairing that a `div` of spans would not. %>
      <dl class="space-y-1 text-sm">
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4" data-figure="rules-need">
          <dt class="text-gray-600">Your rules need</dt>
          <dd class="font-semibold text-gray-900 tabular-nums">
            <%= number_to_currency(presenter.rules_need) %> a period
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4" data-figure="typical-income">
          <dt class="text-gray-600">You typically bring in</dt>
          <dd class="font-semibold text-gray-900 tabular-nums">
            <%= number_to_currency(presenter.typical_income) %> a period
          </dd>
        </div>
        <%# THE THIRD LINE SAYS WHICH WAY IT WENT. §8 prints "Left over $732 → buffer" on a budget
            that fits; the same row on one that does not would print "Left over -$468", which is a
            sum, not a sentence. The underwater wording is §9's own ("$468 underwater every
            period") so the block and the button beneath it name one thing. %>
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4 pt-2 border-t border-gray-100"
             data-figure="leftover">
          <% if presenter.underwater? %>
            <dt class="text-status-danger">Underwater</dt>
            <dd class="font-semibold text-status-danger tabular-nums">
              <%= number_to_currency(-presenter.leftover) %> a period
            </dd>
          <% else %>
            <dt class="text-gray-600">Left over</dt>
            <dd class="font-semibold text-status-success tabular-nums">
              <%= number_to_currency(presenter.leftover) %> → buffer
            </dd>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <%# §9: A PERMANENTLY VISIBLE BUTTON, AND ONLY WHEN INCOME < RULES. Reallocation cannot fix
          a budget that does not fit an income, so this is the one screen that must not offer
          moving money as the answer.

          The target 404s until Task 9 draws `/sacrifice`. Deliberate for this task's window: a
          disabled control or a `href="#"` would be a button that lies about being a button, and
          this one's whole job is to say a real thing is reachable. A literal path rather than a
          route helper because the helper does not exist yet and the page must still render.

          Home's standing-band button class for class, plus a fill — the two are the same offer
          made from two screens and must not look like two different controls. `hover:bg-white`
          rather than `hover:bg-status-danger`: the status colours are custom.css classes and not
          Tailwind utilities, so Tailwind generates no hover variant of them and that hover would
          have silently done nothing. %>
      <% if presenter.underwater? %>
        <%= link_to("What could you cut?",
                    "/sacrifice",
                    class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded border " \
                           "border-status-danger bg-status-danger-light text-status-danger " \
                           "text-sm font-medium hover:bg-white",
                    data: { sacrifice_link: true }) %>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <%# NOTHING TO CHECK YET, and said as an invitation rather than as an error. This is every
          real user's first sight of the block: the figures below are not missing, they are
          unasked. Named in the same words the form's own fields use so the sentence points at
          the controls directly under it. %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 max-w-xl">
        Tell us how long a period is and what you typically bring in, and this will say whether
        your rules fit — and by how much they miss if they don't.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render "declaration_form", presenter: presenter %>
  </div>
</section>
